# Build output — the bundle is generated by build_app.sh, never committed
*.app/
dist/
build/

# Generated icon artifacts (make_icon.py rebuilds these on first build)
src/assets/AppIcon.icns
src/assets/AppIcon.iconset/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# macOS
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes

# Editors
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# Runtime state — lives in ~/Library/Application Support/Card Studio,
# but guard against anyone symlinking or copying it into the tree
settings.json
port
launch.log
# Anchored to the repo root. Unanchored, `designs/` matched examples/founder-card/designs/
# too — and a negation cannot re-include a file inside an ignored DIRECTORY, so the
# example's artwork silently failed to stage while everything beside it committed fine.
/designs/
/output/
*.pdf

# Local scratch
tmp/
scratch/
_site/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Images: DENY BY DEFAULT, then re-allow the folders whose images are meant to
# be public. This is the one rule here that is not about tidiness.
#
# This repo is public and it lives inside a private working tree, and the images
# that pass through that tree are photographs: cards on a desk, a reader, a
# workbench — and card designs carry PEOPLE. One of the designs this app printed
# has a child's name and face on it. `git add .`, `git add -A` and `git commit -a`
# would each have swept any of that into a public commit, because before this
# block there was no image rule of any kind. Publishing a minor's photograph is
# not a mistake you get to fix by deleting the file afterwards; the commit, the
# fork, and the CDN copy all outlive the deletion.
#
# So: no image is committable unless its directory is named below. Adding a new
# public image is a deliberate act — add its path here, and look at it first.
# Deny-by-default is what keeps the accident impossible rather than unlikely.
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.heic
*.heif
*.gif
*.webp
*.bmp
*.tif
*.tiff
*.avif
*.jxl
*.dng
*.raw
*.cr2
*.nef
*.psd
*.svg
*.mp4
*.mov
*.m4v
*.avi
*.webm

# A SAVED DESIGN IS A CARRIER TOO, and it is the one that actually holds the photo.
# `.card.json` embeds its images as base64 — the design behind the card that started
# this project is 481 KB, of which 476 KB is a photograph of a child. An image
# denylist that does not cover the app's own document format protects the file the
# photo arrived as and not the file it ends up in. Designs are user data and belong
# in ~/Library/Application Support/Card Studio/, never here.
*.card.json

# Re-allowed: documentation screenshots, the hardware render, and the app icon set.
# Every entry names an EXTENSION, not a whole tree — `!docs/img/**` would re-admit a
# .card.json or a video dropped in that directory, which would quietly undo the block
# above. Adding a new public image means adding a line here and looking at the file.
!docs/img/*.png
!hardware/**/*.png
!src/assets/icons/*.png
!src/assets/icon_preview.png

# The worked example: a real, printable card kit. Its front face carries a
# photograph of the repo owner, published deliberately — it is his own card and the
# point of it is to be handed to people. Extensions again, not a whole tree.
!examples/**/*.png
!examples/**/*.svg
!examples/**/*.pdf
